00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:50,960
In English, it's called the Responsible Scaling Policy.

246
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:53,530
In Chinese, it's called the Responsible Scaling Policy.

247
00:08:53,530 --> 00:08:55,070
Anthropic has made a public commitment,

248
00:08:55,070 --> 00:08:57,190
When an AI model's capabilities reach a certain threshold,

249
00:08:57,190 --> 00:08:59,350
it will automatically halt its expansion.

250
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:01,720
until it can be proven that this capability is safe.

251
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:04,040
In plain English, Anthropic has made a public promise.

252
00:09:04,040 --> 00:09:05,100
We've made it too powerful.

253
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:06,860
But, we'll hit the brakes ourselves.

254
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:08,840
And once again, they're the first in the industry to do so.

255
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:09,760
Here's what they said, word for word:

256
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:11,900
We want this policy to become the industry standard.

257
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:15,220
All AI companies should publicly make this same commitment.

258
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:17,300
Now, pay attention to that phrase: 'become the industry standard'.

259
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:18,320
And that's the key.

260
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:19,660
Now, let's get back to Sach's perspective.

261
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:22,319
Anthropic is actually calling for industry regulation.

262
00:09:22,319 --> 00:09:24,399
But it would only be binding on large models.

263
00:09:24,399 --> 00:09:25,439
But it's lenient towards smaller companies.

264
00:09:25,439 --> 00:09:27,900
Because smaller companies haven't reached that capability threshold yet.

265
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:31,480
In other words, the regulatory standards are drawn right at Anthropic's capability level.

266
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:32,780
So what about the competitors below that line?

267
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:34,960
are left unchecked.

268
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:36,200
And there's another piece to this puzzle.

269
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:37,560
That's February 2026.

270
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:38,440
Three months ago,

271
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:40,580
Anthropic updated its policy to version 3.0.

272
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:43,180
They publicly admitted that their original promise of an automatic pause...

273
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:45,200
ran into some problems in practice.

274
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:47,460
The reason they gave was the collective action problem.

275
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:49,540
In plain English, it means if we're the only ones who pause,

276
00:09:49,540 --> 00:09:50,340
and our rivals don't pause,

277
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:51,490
and we'd be at a disadvantage.

278
00:09:51,490 --> 00:09:54,250
So, the promise to pause was, well, paused.

279
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:58,300
Three years later, we were also eager to hit the brakes.

280
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:01,590
But if others aren't hitting the brakes, then we're not going to either.

281
00:10:01,590 --> 00:10:03,050
The promise was flexible.

282
00:10:03,050 --> 00:10:05,290
This is what Sachs sees as the most compelling piece of evidence.

283
00:10:05,290 --> 00:10:08,980
The third point is that the CEO has testified before Congress multiple times.

284
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:10,880
The CEO of Anthropic is Dario.

