The reason? OpenAI had violated that very clause.

362
00:13:02,139 --> 00:13:05,150
He used Claude to run a benchmark test against GPT-5.

363
00:13:05,150 --> 00:13:08,210
But the key to this whole thing isn't the ban itself, it's the fact that they wrote the clause back in June.

364
00:13:08,210 --> 00:13:09,920
And by August, they were already using it.

365
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:11,700
A clause that didn't even exist a month ago.

366
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:14,000
And just a month later, it became the very proof used to justify the ban.

367
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,980
This is a classic example of someone who makes the rules.

368
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:18,220
And proof that they're also the ones who enforce them.

369
00:13:18,220 --> 00:13:19,940
Violation of the new terms! On August 1st, Sach posted a direct statement on his blog.

370
00:13:19,940 --> 00:13:21,320
This is anti-competitive behavior.

371
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:25,130
Can you imagine why Anthropic would ban OpenAI from using its models?

372
00:13:25,130 --> 00:13:27,230
